WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:04.880
John Carpenter's Halloween gave birth to the
modern slasher film, establishing virtually

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.530
every one of the genre's tropes and dramatically
announcing its director as a major new talent.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:12.999
But even though you've seen it a dozen times,
there's a lot you might not know about one

00:00:12.999 --> 00:00:17.230
of the greatest and most influential horror
films ever made.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:19.880
Independent feature
It's now a classic horror film that paved

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.740
the way for everything that came after, but
Halloween wasn't exactly a major production.

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.820
The film was the brainchild of producer Irwin
Yablans who hired Carpenter because of his

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:32.140
strong visual style and ability to innovate
on a tight budget.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.000
Yablans' idea was simple enough:
"Ah, a movie about babysitters in jeopardy

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.140
being terrorized and we'll do it all in one
night."

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:44.170
In fact, early in pre-production, the project
had the working title The Babysitter Murders.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.500
Speaking with Entertainment Weekly, Carpenter
remembered,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.970
"I was finishing [directing] a TV movie called
Someone's Watching Me! when Irwin Yablans

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:55.720
called and said, 'Why don't we set it on Halloween
night, in fact, why don't we call it Halloween?'"

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.540
Carpenter came up with the idea of the small
town with a dark secret that's revisited on

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.540
Halloween night.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.540
He and Debra Hill wrote the script, as Hill
explained to Fangoria:

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.610
"We wrote the whole thing in three weeks.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.290
Then we just had to find the right actors."

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:11.240
Casting Laurie
Especially important was casting the part

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.220
of Laurie Strode, the movie's heroine.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:17.400
Tommy Lee Wallace, Carpenter's friend and
one of the film's editors, explained to Consequence

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.890
of Sound:
"This was a time that women were asserting

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.460
their rights like never before, and Debra
was a very assertive woman.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.070
No way she was going to have a weeping violet
type as her heroine."

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:30.750
Carpenter, who didn't watch television, had
never seen Jamie Lee Curtis' work on the series

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.960
Operation Petticoat, but Hill was convinced
she'd be perfect for the role.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.920
One small detail was all it took to convince
Carpenter, Curtis also happened to be the

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.530
daughter of Janet Leigh, perhaps the original
scream queen, whose shower had been rudely

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.470
interrupted in the 1960 classic Psycho.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:51.420
Pulling out all the tricks
The film was shot with a $320,000 budget.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.640
If it doesn't look particularly low-budget,
it's because Carpenter was a master at making

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.150
something from nothing.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.280
As there was no room in the budget for a costume
department, the entire cast's wardrobe was

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.890
purchased on the cheap at local department
stores.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:08.729
Michael Myers' mask was crafted from a repurposed
William Shatner mask, purchased from a local

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.390
costume store for $1.98 and painted white.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:16.090
Also enabling them to keep the budget in check,
the filming location for the Myers' house

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.900
was an abandoned property, owned by a church.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.360
And although the film was shot in Pasadena,
California, it was set in Illinois, so Carpenter

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.420
evoked the feel of autumn in the Midwest by
using the same several trash bags full of

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.840
painted leaves for exterior scenes, scattering
them around whatever street they were currently

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.810
shooting on, only to collect them all to be
re-used in the next shot.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:40.430
The minimalist aesthetic carried over to post-production;
Carpenter wrote the film's classic theme in

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.810
only an hour, and scored the entire film himself
in just three days.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:47.580
Working for peanuts
Another way to keep the budget low was to

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:48.849
pay very little.

00:02:48.849 --> 00:02:53.710
As the biggest name in the cast, Donald Pleasence
got a flat fee of $20,000, while little-known

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:56.760
star Curtis received only $8,000.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:01.560
Carpenter's buddy Nick Castle was paid the
princely sum of $25 per day to portray "The

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.600
Shape," as Michael Myers is called in the
credits, and Carpenter himself took only $10,000

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.510
plus a small percentage of the film's profits,
which ended up working out for him.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:13.090
He said:
"I can't remember exactly how much money I

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:14.090
made off the film.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:18.209
I do remember getting one check for over a
million dollars, that wasn't bad."

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:22.640
Limited release
Halloween debuted on October 25, 1978 in a

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.290
single city, Kansas City, Missouri.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.690
As the buzz started to grow, national critics
started paying attention.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:32.830
The great Roger Ebert concluded his sparkling
review with a warning that should have had

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:35.490
any horror fan sprinting out the door to buy
tickets:

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.580
"I'd like to be clear about this.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:42.019
If you don't want to have a really terrifying
experience, don't see Halloween."

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:46.730
The film went on to gross an astonishing $47
million, reigning for over a decade as the

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.550
most profitable independent film ever made.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.900
Not as gory as you think
Despite how you might remember it, Halloween

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.790
includes virtually no onscreen gore.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:04:00.420
Carpenter's expert use of slowly building
tension, spooky imagery, and perfectly deployed

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.400
jump scares left audiences with the impression
that what they had just seen must have been

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.099
crazy violent in order to be that disturbing.

00:04:07.099 --> 00:04:08.600
But that wasn't Carpenter's take.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:13.890
"I wanted something deadly simple and something
that would just eat at you a little bit."

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:18.109
Hill alluded to this in production notes for
1981's Halloween 2, saying,

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:22.180
"People don't seem to realize that we showed
next to no blood in the first picture.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.900
You think you're seeing a lot more than we're
showing you."

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.389
The almost-anthology
Carpenter and Hill never envisioned even a

00:04:28.389 --> 00:04:30.969
single sequel, let alone a franchise.

00:04:30.969 --> 00:04:34.199
Carpenter explained to Deadline:
"I didn't think there was any more story,

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:35.659
and I didn't want to do it again."

00:04:35.659 --> 00:04:39.949
Instead of finding ways to bring Michael Myers
back, Carpenter and Hill had the idea to tell

00:04:39.949 --> 00:04:44.879
a different story with each new Halloween
film, making it an ongoing horror anthology

00:04:44.879 --> 00:04:45.999
series.

00:04:45.999 --> 00:04:51.339
Halloween 2's tepid box office convinced producers
to give it a shot, and the result, 1982's

00:04:51.339 --> 00:04:55.259
Halloween 3: Season of the Witch, instantly
polarized horror fans everywhere.

00:04:55.259 --> 00:04:59.479
Many fans wanted Michael Myers back and they
got their wish six years later, although the

00:04:59.479 --> 00:05:03.430
sequels and rebooted series that followed
provided mostly diminishing returns, with

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:04.949
one exception.

00:05:04.949 --> 00:05:09.849
Halloween H20: 20 Years Later ignored previous
sequels and served as a direct sequel to Halloween

00:05:09.849 --> 00:05:10.849
2.

00:05:10.849 --> 00:05:14.039
Ironically, Halloween is set to receive the
same retcon treatment again.

00:05:14.039 --> 00:05:18.779
The Halloween reboot scheduled for 2018 will
serve as a direct sequel to the original,

00:05:18.779 --> 00:05:23.249
with Carpenter serving as executive producer
and providing the score, and Jamie Lee Curtis

00:05:23.249 --> 00:05:25.699
returning to play Laurie Strode one more time.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.300
"Michaaaaeeeel."

